

**14. Coulomb Wave Functions**

**Mathematical Properties**

**14.1. Differential Equation, Series Expansions**

**Differential Equation**

**14.1.1**

\[\frac{d^{2}w}{d\rho^{2}}+[1-\frac{2\eta}{\rho}\,\frac{L(L+1)}{\rho^{2}}]w=0\]

(\(\rho>\)0, \(-\infty<\eta<\infty\), \(L\) a non-negative integer)

The Coulomb wave equation has a regular singularity at \(\rho=0\) with indices \(L+1\) and \(-L\); it has an irregular singularity at \(\rho=\infty\).

**General Solution**

**14.1.2**

\[w=C_{1}F_{L}(\eta,\ \rho)+C_{2}G_{2}(\eta,\ \rho)\qquad(C_{1},\ C_{2}\ \text{ constants})\]

where \(F_{L}(\eta,\ \rho)\) is the regular Coulomb wave function and \(G_{L}(\eta,\ \rho)\) is the irregular (logarithmic) Coulomb wave function.

**Regular Coulomb Wave Function \(F_{L}(\eta,\ \rho)\)**

**14.1.3**

\[F_{L}(\eta,\ \rho)=C_{L}(\eta)\,\rho^{L+1}e^{-t\rho}M(L+1-i\eta,\,2L+2,\,2 i\rho)\]

**14.1.4**

\[=C_{L}(\eta)\,\rho^{L+1}\Phi_{L}(\eta,\,\rho)\]

**14.1.5**

\[\Phi_{L}(\eta,\ \rho)=\sum\limits_{k=L+1}^{\infty}A_{k}^{L}(\eta)\,\rho^{k-L-1}\]

**14.1.6**

\[A_{L+1}^{L}=1,\ \ A_{L+2}^{L}=\frac{\eta}{L+1},\]

\[(k+L)(k-L-1)A_{k}^{L}=2\eta A_{k-1}^{L}-A_{k-2}^{L}\quad(k>L+2)\]

**14.1.7**

\[C_{L}(\eta)=\frac{2^{L}e^{-\frac{\pi\eta}{2}}|\Gamma(L+1+i\eta)|}{\Gamma(2L+2)}\]

(See chapter 6.)

**14.1.8**

\[C_{2}^{\alpha}(\eta)=2\pi\eta(e^{\pi\eta}-1)^{-1}\]

**14.1.9**

\[C_{L}^{\alpha}(\eta)=\frac{p_{L}(\eta)C_{2}^{\alpha}(\eta)}{2\eta(2L+1)}\]

**14.1.10**

\[C_{L}(\eta)=\frac{(L^{+}\eta^{2})!}{L(2L+1)!}\,C_{L-1}(\eta)\]

**14.1.11**

\[\frac{p_{L}(\eta)}{2\eta}=\frac{(1+\eta)(4+\eta^{2})\,\ldots\,(L^{2}+\eta^{2} )2^{2\epsilon}}{(2L+1)[(2L)!]^{2}}\]